#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3edf2dc-e079-57e5-96d2-8bce38a9f836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368850} {:answer/id #uuid "1385ec7d-713a-55c8-92da-323cdfcb2ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368851} {:answer/id #uuid "2a17dbb6-9043-5b4d-92cb-700ffcbb8d7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368852} {:answer/id #uuid "612a3ec9-d348-55f6-83af-9b4271e4b630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368853} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc2081b-9590-5720-b802-00c42e516f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1385ec7d-713a-55c8-92da-323cdfcb2ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368851} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd4a38bf-1f11-59a4-a2b9-6037f8331898" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07957b89-f6f1-537f-ad10-787ac05c390f" :question/math-academy-id "q-100091" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1-1)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(1-1)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2bcfcb6-8f8b-5aa9-8346-05db880fa5bb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Polynomials is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e388b22-5e8d-5396-ae32-978dd6ad14ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368857} {:answer/id #uuid "adc2afe7-960c-51bc-aefd-178846c7b056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368858} {:answer/id #uuid "54bdfaeb-dce4-5299-8d37-57c62e3f1c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368859} {:answer/id #uuid "51a3a06c-9bbc-52c5-a0d5-925563e8330a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements is true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368860} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5ce868-8739-56b4-bc89-a6ed86321de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54bdfaeb-dce4-5299-8d37-57c62e3f1c90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368859} :answer-field/id #uuid "5546d092-75d2-5847-909d-f560bed58c1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98763a63-2338-5cf6-99f3-477072c178b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-100119" :question/problem "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-2)q(-2)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot ((-2)+2)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2-2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t+2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t+2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-2)+2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2+2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =32\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d833b67-9387-5350-a117-a8fb820c8999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368863} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d3f22b-0f81-5ab7-9dcf-77fb5ba56c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368864} {:answer/id #uuid "12ab2596-b6ed-5ea2-8b39-f362a0c4c492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368865} {:answer/id #uuid "e45f7a00-56e3-5bb0-b128-6960ccbba81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368866} {:answer/id #uuid "6804d3f0-e093-58ee-ac8e-938e15db5b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d833b67-9387-5350-a117-a8fb820c8999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368863} :answer-field/id #uuid "becc253a-1312-5974-a8b2-8f83981104cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a5307a8-8a58-5d9f-8aa3-c19ff22a65ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-98524" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[0,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t-t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =0+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4116bad4-7704-5d3c-aead-f4e88e539e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368870} {:answer/id #uuid "d46e0d50-1dfa-5ae7-a1f4-0e084a87d49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368871} {:answer/id #uuid "c7817704-2c2c-5c82-b726-44f05a57b617" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368872} {:answer/id #uuid "03deeae6-3174-507a-874c-fa238c78bfd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368873} {:answer/id #uuid "600d6f02-be35-5d88-88d5-edbe61169d3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d46e0d50-1dfa-5ae7-a1f4-0e084a87d49a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368871} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0197e2d-78e4-59b1-866d-0326af420662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368869}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf274c2e-03ff-5221-83a7-3ad5b6f8168e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99827" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=1,\\,{f}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c87e8ada-6876-5f3b-aef3-f4132922cfb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368877} {:answer/id #uuid "532611c8-25ed-5d0e-b24b-ff6b277154b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368878} {:answer/id #uuid "e085f337-a139-5495-a885-f22a8b50d1d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368879} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa87ef-d443-57f2-b170-104896a37179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368880} {:answer/id #uuid "d15a36c7-be51-5acc-aa89-16a7cb924587" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "532611c8-25ed-5d0e-b24b-ff6b277154b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368878} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1ca5951-ba3c-5cda-bd2b-1de4dbbfd8d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "14911b6b-4e1d-55bf-aff2-fa02f5f045e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-99848" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-2,2]$ of continuous functions over $[-2,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-2)^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}t\\cdot (t-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}({t}^{2}-\\frac{5}{12}{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{t}^{5}){|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}{2}^{5}-(\\frac{(-2)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}(-2)^{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t),$ ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ and ${f}_{2}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
